{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785183896,"nonce":"ry+tiGgzEXJz/QXTtaE7o4e8IDw=","title":"ADR-0062 s7: optional node-side write accelerator — local upper, network lower","message":"Opt-in, for write-heavy Steps on nodes with fast local disk. A privileged Scarab-owned **native sidecar** mounts `overlayfs` with the **Export as lowerdir (read-only)** and a node-local `emptyDir` as **upperdir**, and propagates the merged view to `/workspace`. Reads still fault over the network; **writes never touch it during the Step**; at exit the sidecar ships the upper layer — the exact change set — to the service.\n\nMechanics already measured on colima (ADR-0062): `Bidirectional` requires a privileged container but the step container stays unprivileged with `HostToContainer`, and the mount **stacks on the shared volume's root**, so `/workspace` does not move. Also measured: **unmount does not propagate back**, so a dead daemon leaves a live mountpoint failing every operation — that must become a named Attempt failure.\n\nThis is the same primitive in a different place, not a second architecture: same Farm, same CAS, same roots, same `/workspace`, and in both cases the upper layer is the change set.\n\n\n- [ ] Opt-in per Environment/Step, fail-closed when not permitted, and refused cleanly where PodSecurity forbids it\n- [ ] Writes during the Step generate no network traffic to the service\n- [ ] The change set shipped at exit is identical to what the service-side overlay would have produced for the same work\n- [ ] A dead sidecar fails the Attempt with a stated reason\n- [ ] Node loss mid-Step cannot mark the Attempt `Succeeded` (ADR-0061 part 4)\n- [ ] `overlayfs` with an NFS mount as `lowerdir` verified rather than assumed — currently an ADR-0062 open item\n\n\n- s6 (the Export is the lower layer)","files":null}]}